# Copy to .env, or let ./setup.sh write it for you.
#
# **This file is small on purpose.** Every repository still keeps its own .env -
# that is what `make run` and `pnpm dev` read on the host, and copying those
# keys here would be a second copy to keep in agreement. docker-compose.yml
# loads each of them with `env_file`.
#
# What belongs here is only what compose interpolates itself: a volume path, a
# published port, a build argument, a subnet, and the secrets of the services
# that load no repository's .env. Nothing in here reaches a container except by
# being named again in the `environment` block of a service.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- required --

# One root for every tenant: `<TWIN_ROOT>/<company>/<account>`, with the
# company's `shared` beside it. Mounted into the engine at the same absolute
# path it has here, because a `cwd` the twin hands to `sessions.spawn` is a host
# path to the Claude Code agent and a container path to the engine - and they
# have to be the same string or every spawn lands somewhere that does not exist.
#
# Absolute, and on this machine. A copied .env almost always points at the
# previous machine's home directory.
TWIN_ROOT=

# Inlined into the frontend's client bundle by `next build`, so compose needs it
# as a build argument and `env_file` cannot supply it. Publishable by design -
# it is the key the browser presents - so an image layer holding it gives
# nothing away. Keep it identical to the one in twin-frontend/.env.local.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# What the engine presents to the sandbox broker, and what the broker presents
# back when it ships its audit records. Both services are named in
# docker-compose.yml, so this is their one copy. ./setup.sh mints both.
TWIN_SANDBOX_BROKER_TOKEN=
TWIN_SANDBOX_AUDIT_TOKEN=

# What the coding boxes' proxies present to the engine, and the model key the
# LLM proxy forwards with (twin-engine SANDBOX-PLAN phase 8). Here and not
# through `env_file`, because a box can reach those proxies and they must hold
# nothing else. ./setup.sh mints the first and copies the second from
# twin-engine/.env.
TWIN_BOX_PROXY_TOKEN=
OPENROUTER_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- optional --
# Every one of these has a working default. Uncomment to move it.

# Host ports. The containers always talk to each other on the standard ports -
# `postgres:5432`, `redis:6379` - because only the *host* side moves.
#POSTGRES_PORT=5500
#REDIS_PORT=6500
#API_PORT=8080
#FRONTEND_PORT=4000
#TWIN_API_PORT=8000
#MEMORY_API_PORT=8200
# These two are published on 127.0.0.1 only. The preview port is also in every
# task card's link, `http://<box>.<port>.preview.localhost:<port>`, so moving it
# moves the link with it.
#TEMPORAL_UI_PORT=8233
#TWIN_PREVIEW_PORT=47620

# The one origin CORS allows, and where the browser is sent after connecting.
# localhost:4000 and 127.0.0.1:4000 are different origins to a browser, and
# getting it wrong is total and silent: every credentialed call refused, the
# event stream never opening. Match what you type in the address bar.
#FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:4000

# The public url a provider dials to deliver a webhook. Loopback is correct
# until you start the tunnel below; with it running, this is the tunnel's
# hostname, and the OAuth redirect uri is built from it too.
#BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Cloudflare > Networks > Tunnels. Needed only by `--profile tunnel`, whose
# route should point at http://backend:8080 on the compose network.
#CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# The Claude Code agent is a host process, not a container, so connectors
# reaches it through Docker's alias for the host.
#CLAUDE_CODE_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:47600

# Which instance this is, for the bar at the foot of every page. The frontend's
# `environment` block wins over its .env.local, so this is where it is moved.
#TWIN_ENV=dev
# twin-connectors' own mode, and nothing else's.
#NODE_ENV=development

# How twin-memory embeds. `none` is lexical-only and needs no model, which is
# what this container can actually run: the mlx backend is Apple-silicon-only,
# and the alternative pulls sentence-transformers into the image.
#TWIN_EMBEDDER=none

# What twin-memory spends. Every Nth batch, and whenever the queue goes quiet,
# settled threads are carded - the one paid stage on the write path; 0 turns it
# off. The budget is per UTC day, kept in Redis so a restart does not reset it.
#MEMORY_CARD_EVERY=10
#MEMORY_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=5

# Every network is pinned off 172.19/16, this office's LAN (172.19.2.0/23).
# Compose picked a subnet on its own once and took the LAN, which routed every
# LAN address onto the bridge. twin-engine/docs/ports.md is the register - move
# one here and there together.
#TWIN_SUBNET=10.201.0.0/16
#TWIN_EGRESS_SUBNET=10.203.0.0/24
#TWIN_SANDBOX_NETWORK_POOL=10.204.0.0/16
#TWIN_BOX_SERVICES_SUBNET=10.206.0.0/24
#TWIN_PREVIEW_SUBNET=10.207.0.0/24

# What tenant containers, their egress proxy and coding boxes are made from.
# ./setup.sh builds all three. `runsc` runs boxes under gVisor, on a Linux host
# that has it; empty is Docker's default runtime.
#TWIN_SANDBOX_IMAGE=twin-sandbox:dev
#TWIN_EGRESS_IMAGE=twin-egress:dev
#TWIN_BOX_IMAGE=twin-box:dev
#TWIN_BOX_RUNTIME=

# The hosts an account's own container may reach beside those its person
# granted. Setting it replaces the default list in docker-compose.yml rather
# than adding to it. Coding boxes reach any public host on 443 whatever it says.
#TWIN_EGRESS_ALLOWLIST=

# Langfuse comes up with this stack but on a network of its own, so it is reached
# across the host at its published port. Docker Desktop resolves the name by
# itself; a Linux engine does not, so set this there. Langfuse's own subnet and
# secrets are in twin-engine/infra/langfuse/.env, not here.
#LANGFUSE_HOST=http://host.docker.internal:3000
